Posterior shoulder tissue strain — Borstad and Dashottar (@med-source-capsule-study: 0, is #reference, #part-of @medicine-sources) { r_6XDNYGV5BX07M2TJYPE3XSR8AJ
John D. Borstad and Amitabh Dashottar. Quantifying strain on posterior shoulder tissues during 5 simulated clinical tests: A cadaver study. Journal of Orthopaedic & Sports Physical Therapy 41, 2011, pages 90–99. DOI 10.2519/jospt.2011.3357. English.

[Free author manuscript](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3032833/).

Read Abstract first, then Methods, Results, and the limitations paragraphs near the end of Discussion. The experiment compares defined tissue conditions and positions in cadaver specimens. Inspect sample, tissue preparation, scapular fixation, loading, and strain measurement before interpreting a result. It measures mechanical behavior, not pain or diagnostic accuracy in a living patient.

For advanced study, read the complete manuscript and references. The paper tests a hypothesis rather than validating a universal end-range threshold, one self-test, or a stretching prescription.

**Access and reuse:** freely readable author manuscript in PMC. No unrestricted adaptation licence is asserted and no copy is supplied; use the article's copyright notice. It is included for its anatomical experiment and limitations, not as current management guidance.

